<p>This does not mean you should only ask for a phone number on every page. It means you should match the form to the context. A homeowner reading a blog post about roof costs might give you their email for a cost guide. A homeowner on your emergency plumbing page at 10 PM will only give you their phone number. Match the capture mechanism to the intent.</p>

<h3>Speed to Lead: The 5-Minute Rule</h3>

<p>Here is a statistic that should change how you think about lead response: <strong>Contractors who contact leads within 5 minutes are 21x more likely to qualify the lead than those who wait 30 minutes.</strong> That is not a typo. Twenty-one times.</p>

<p>The reason is simple. Homeowners submit 3-5 forms when they need a contractor. The first one to call gets the conversation. The second one gets voicemail. The third one gets ignored. Speed is not a nice-to-have. It is the single biggest competitive advantage in lead conversion.</p>

<p>This means:</p>

<ul>
<li>Lead notifications must go to a phone, not an email inbox.</li>
<li>Someone must be assigned to respond immediately, even after hours.</li>
<li>Auto-responders are not enough. A text auto-reply buys you 10 minutes. A phone call within 5 minutes wins the lead.</li>
<li>If you cannot respond within 5 minutes consistently, you need a call answering service or a dedicated inside sales rep.</li>
</ul>

<p>An HVAC contractor in Houston implemented a 5-minute response guarantee using a combination of automated text replies and a dedicated call center. Their lead-to-appointment rate increased from 18% to 41%. They did not get more leads. They converted the same leads at more than double the rate.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Nurture — Staying Top-of-Mind Until the Homeowner Is Ready to Buy</h2>

<p>Not every lead is ready to buy today. In fact, most are not. A homeowner researching a kitchen remodel might be 6-12 months from making a decision. A homeowner whose AC is making a weird noise might wait until it breaks completely. A homeowner who got an estimate for a roof replacement might need to wait for insurance approval or save for a down payment.</p>

<p>If your only follow-up is a single phone call and then silence, you are leaving 80% of your potential revenue on the table. Nurture is the system that keeps you top-of-mind during the long gap between interest and decision.</p>

<h3>The Three Types of Nurture Campaigns</h3>

<p>We organize nurture into three categories based on where the lead is in their journey:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Immediate Nurture (0-14 days).</strong> High frequency, high value. This is for leads who just requested information. A sequence of 5-7 touchpoints across text, email, and phone. Each touchpoint adds value: answers to common questions, a video from the owner, a case study of a similar project, a checklist for evaluating contractors.</li>
<li><strong>Active Nurture (15-90 days).</strong> Medium frequency, educational. For leads who are interested but not ready. Weekly emails with helpful content. Retargeting ads that show recent projects. A monthly "What is happening in [city] home services" update.</li>
<li><strong>Long-Term Nurture (90+ days).</strong> Low frequency, relationship-focused. Monthly or quarterly emails. Seasonal reminders ("Time for your pre-winter furnace check"). Birthday or home-anniversary messages. Annual market updates.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What to Send: Content That Actually Helps</h3>

<p>The biggest mistake in nurture is sending "just checking in" emails. No one wants to be checked in on. They want value. Here is what works:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>The Decision Guide.</strong> A downloadable PDF that walks homeowners through how to evaluate contractors for their specific project. Not a sales pitch. A genuine guide. It positions you as the expert and subtly frames the criteria in your favor.</li>
<li><strong>The Case Study Series.</strong> Before-and-after stories sent via email, one per week. "See how we solved a [specific problem] for a homeowner in [neighborhood]." Specificity builds trust.</li>
<li><strong>The FAQ Video Series.</strong> Short videos (60-90 seconds) answering common questions. "How long does a roof replacement really take?" "What happens if you find mold during a bathroom remodel?" Video builds connection faster than text.</li>
<li><strong>The Seasonal Reminder.</strong> Timely, relevant, and helpful. "Hurricane season is here. Here is your 5-point roof inspection checklist." "Your furnace has been working hard. Here is how to know if it needs attention before winter."</li>
<li><strong>The Social Proof Update.</strong> "We just completed our 500th project in [city]. Here is what we have learned." Milestones build credibility.</li>
</ol>

<h3>The Technology of Nurture</h3>

<p>You do not need enterprise software to nurture effectively. Here is a simple tech stack that works:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>CRM:</strong> Jobber, ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, or HubSpot. The key feature is automated email and text sequences triggered by lead status.</li>
<li><strong>Email platform:</strong> Mailchimp, ActiveCampaign, or the built-in email tools in your CRM. You need automation, segmentation, and basic analytics.</li>
<li><strong>Retargeting:</strong> Facebook and Google retargeting pixels on your website. Show ads to visitors who did not convert. Cost is low, and you are only advertising to people who already know you.</li>
<li><strong>Text messaging:</strong> A platform that allows automated and manual texts. Open rates for contractor texts are 95%+. Use them wisely, not spammy.</li>
</ul>

<p>A remodeling contractor in Seattle built a 90-day nurture sequence for kitchen remodel leads. The sequence included 12 emails, 4 text messages, and 2 direct mail pieces. Of leads who entered the sequence, 34% eventually booked a consultation. Of those who did not enter the sequence (the control group), only 9% eventually booked. Nurture tripled their conversion rate from the same lead volume.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Amplify — Turning Every Customer into a Referral and Review Engine</h2>

<p>The final step of the Flywheel is where the magic happens. Amplify is the system that turns every completed job into future leads. It is how the flywheel becomes self-reinforcing. When Amplify works, your cost per lead drops over time because your best leads come from your best customers.</p>

<p>Most contractors treat referrals and reviews as happy accidents. They happen when a customer is especially pleased, or when the contractor happens to remember to ask. That is not a system. That is hope. And hope is not a strategy.</p>

<h3>The Referral System: Making Asking Automatic</h3>

<p>Referrals happen when three conditions are met:</p>

<ol>
<li>The customer is genuinely satisfied.</li>
<li>The customer is reminded that referrals are valuable to you.</li>
<li>The customer has a simple, low-friction way to make the referral.</li>
</ol>

<p>Here is a referral system that works:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>At project completion:</strong> Hand the homeowner a printed card with a QR code. "We grow through referrals from happy customers like you. Scan this to send $100 to a friend who needs [service]. They will get $100 off their project, and you will get $100 when they book."</li>
<li><strong>One week after completion:</strong> Send a text: "Hi [Name], how is everything holding up? If you know anyone who needs [service], we would love to help them too. Here is a link to share: [link]."</li>
<li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> Email past customers with a seasonal offer. "Spring is here. Know anyone whose roof took a beating this winter? We are offering free inspections for referrals."</li>
<li><strong>Annually:</strong> Send a handwritten thank-you note on the anniversary of their project. No ask. Just gratitude. Gratitude creates reciprocity.</li>
</ul>

<p>A plumbing contractor in Austin implemented this system and generated 47 referrals in one year from a customer base of 312 past clients. Their referral close rate was 68%, compared to 22% for online leads. The cost per referral lead was $12 (the cost of the reward), compared to $87 for paid leads.</p>

<h3>The Review Flywheel: How Reviews Drive Visibility</h3>

<p>Reviews do two things simultaneously. They build Authority (Step 2) and they drive Visibility (Step 3). Google explicitly uses review quantity, velocity, and sentiment as ranking factors in local search. A contractor with 200 recent five-star reviews will outrank a contractor with 50 mixed reviews, all else being equal.</p>

<p>The review system from Step 2 feeds directly into Amplify. Every new review makes you more visible. Every new customer from that visibility becomes a source of future reviews and referrals. This is the flywheel effect in action.</p>

<p>Here is how to close the loop:</p>

<ol>
<li>Deliver a remarkable experience (Foundation + Authority).</li>
<li>Get found by new customers (Visibility).</li>
<li>Convert them into leads (Capture).</li>
<li>Stay in touch until they buy (Nurture).</li>
<li>Turn them into reviewers and referrers (Amplify).</li>
<li>Their reviews and referrals make you more visible (back to Visibility).</li>
</ol>

<p>Each cycle makes the next cycle easier. That is why we call it a flywheel, not a funnel. Funnels require constant new input. Flywheels build momentum.</p>

<h3>Strategic Partnerships: The Overlooked Amplification Channel</h3>

<p>One of the most underutilized Amplify strategies is strategic partnerships. Who already serves your ideal customer before they need you?</p>

<ul>
<li>Roofers partner with real estate agents and home inspectors.</li>
<li>HVAC contractors partner with home warranty companies and energy auditors.</li>
<li>Plumbers partner with general contractors and property managers.</li>
<li>Remodelers partner with interior designers and architects.</li>
</ul>

<p>A strategic partnership is not a casual "send me referrals." It is a structured relationship with clear value exchange. You refer them. They refer you. You co-market. You share content. You appear in each other's email newsletters. A single strong partnership can generate 20-50 qualified leads per year with zero ad spend.</p>

<h2>Case Study: How a $2M Roofing Contractor Built the Flywheel in 18 Months</h2>

<p>To show you what the Flywheel looks like in practice, here is an illustrative walkthrough of how the framework plays out for a typical roofing contractor in the Midwest. Picture a company doing $1.8 million in annual revenue, spending $8,000 per month on Google Ads, and getting inconsistent results. Their cost per lead is $143. Their close rate is 19%. They are profitable but stressed, and they know the current model is not scalable. Here is how the playbook would sequence over 18 months, with the kind of results a contractor in this situation can realistically expect.</p>

<h3>Month 1-3: Foundation and Authority</h3>

<p>The sequence starts without running a single new ad. Instead, the website gets rebuilt from the ground up. Positioning is clarified: "Storm damage insurance specialists for homeowners who want the claim handled, not just the roof replaced." Every service page is rewritten. The Google Business Profile is optimized. A review collection system is implemented.</p>

<p>By month three, a contractor following this sequence could realistically accumulate around 47 new Google reviews, lift website conversion rate from roughly 2.1% to 7.4%, and increase Google Business Profile impressions by 300%+.</p>

<h3>Month 4-9: Visibility and Capture</h3>

<p>With Foundation and Authority in place, the next phase layers on <a href="/blog/local-seo-guide/">local SEO</a> and optimizes the Google Ads account. That means location-specific service pages for every city in the service area, local citations, and a content program answering common insurance claim questions.</p>

<p>In a sequence like this, organic leads can grow from around 4 per month to 23 per month. Google Ads cost per lead would typically drop from $143 to roughly $61 because the improved website and authority signals raise Quality Score and conversion rate. Total lead volume can climb 180% while ad spend stays flat.</p>

<h3>Month 10-18: Nurture and Amplify</h3>

<p>The final phase adds a 90-day email nurture sequence for insurance claim leads, who often take 3-6 months to decide, a referral program with a $250 reward, and partnerships with a handful of local insurance agents and home inspectors.</p>

